{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792793108,"nonce":"ssmXUyLTJqXK1C6oGOBXTGLeQR4=","title":"Python: deltablue's first iteration is compile latency, 30x CPython's","message":"CI's speed table has deltablue 7.3x (Linux) and 6.3x (macOS) slower than CPython: the `--fast` mean is over five iterations and zypy's first one dominates it. NUC, release, ZYPY_LLVM=1, taskset 0-3, iteration ms:\n\n- tiered default: 80 / 4.7 / 0.8 / 0.7 / 0.8, mean 17\n- every function compiled on its first call (ZYNTAX_BASELINE_THRESHOLD=1): 57 / 0.4 / 0.4 / 0.4 / 0.4, mean 12\n- interpreter only (threshold 1e8, OSR and static-hot off): 118 ms every iteration\n- CPython 3.12: 1.7 to 2.6 ms every iteration\n\nSo the steady state is 2.5x to 4x faster than CPython, the interpreter is about 70x slower, and the first iteration is spent compiling: ZYNTAX_TRACE_LAZY shows 153 compiles during it, 65 of them runtime library functions (zb_*, 70 ms of compile time) and 88 program functions (90 ms), across the warm-up and first-call threads. ZYNTAX_OPT_LEVEL=none and ZYNTAX_REGALLOC=single_pass shave the first iteration (80 to 55-60 ms) but not the gap.\n\nBeating CPython here needs the first iteration under about 8 ms: the runtime library compiled ahead of time (it is the same code in every program), and an interpreter within a small factor of CPython's for the program's own cold code.","files":null}]}